    content: `
      <h2>Introduction</h2>
      <p>As a fresh graduate, your resume is the single most important document in your job search. It serves as your primary marketing tool, representing your capabilities, education, and potential to hiring managers before they ever speak to you. With recruiters spending an average of just 6 to 8 seconds scanning a single resume, it is absolutely vital that yours stands out immediately.</p>
      <p>The challenge for freshers is clear: you lack years of professional experience, yet you are competing with hundreds of applicants who have similar educational backgrounds. The differentiator is almost always <strong>how well you present your existing knowledge and projects</strong>. Here are five comprehensive, actionable tips to help you build an impressive fresher resume that lands interviews consistently.</p>

      <h2>1. Choose a Clean, Professional Layout</h2>
      <p>Before a recruiter reads a single word, they notice the design of your resume. Many freshers make the costly mistake of using highly decorative templates with complex sidebars, progress bars for skills, and multiple colors. These layouts often confuse Applicant Tracking Systems (ATS) — the software that filters resumes before human eyes ever see them — and make reading difficult.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Keep it to one page:</strong> As a fresher, you do not have enough experience to justify a two-page resume. Be concise and ruthlessly edit every word.</li>
        <li><strong>Use standard fonts:</strong> Select clean, readable fonts like Inter, Arial, Calibri, or Helvetica in sizes 10 to 12. Avoid decorative or script fonts entirely.</li>
        <li><strong>Use clear section headings:</strong> Separate sections with bold headers (e.g., Education, Projects, Skills, Internships). ATS systems look for these standard keywords.</li>
        <li><strong>Avoid skill progress bars:</strong> Rating your skills "8 out of 10" is subjective and unhelpful. Instead, categorize them as "Proficient," "Familiar," or "Learning."</li>
        <li><strong>Use consistent spacing:</strong> Ensure uniform margins (0.5 to 1 inch) and consistent line spacing throughout the document.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Save and submit your resume as a PDF unless the job application specifically asks for a Word (.docx) file. PDFs preserve your formatting across all devices.</p>

      <h2>2. Write a Tailored Career Objective</h2>
      <p>Located at the top of your resume, the career objective is your elevator pitch. Avoid generic summaries like <em>"Seeking a challenging position in a reputable organization to utilize my skills."</em> Recruiters see this hundreds of times a day — it communicates absolutely nothing about you specifically.</p>
      <p>Instead, write a specific, value-driven statement that references the exact company and role: <em>"Detail-oriented B.Tech Computer Science graduate with strong hands-on experience in React.js and Node.js, developed during a 3-month internship at StartupX. Eager to contribute to building high-performance web applications at TechCorp while collaborating in an agile team environment."</em></p>
      <p>Notice how this version includes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your specific degree and specialization</li>
        <li>A named technology skill relevant to the role</li>
        <li>Reference to real experience (internship)</li>
        <li>The name of the company you are applying to</li>
        <li>A specific contribution you intend to make</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. Highlight Academic Projects with Structure</h2>
      <p>Since you may not have extensive professional work experience, your academic and personal projects are your greatest assets. Treat them like real jobs — they represent your ability to apply knowledge to real problems. Instead of just listing the project name, describe it using the <strong>STAR method (Situation, Task, Action, Result)</strong>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Title &amp; Tech Stack:</strong> Clearly state the project title and the technologies used (e.g., <em>"E-Commerce Platform | React, MongoDB, Express, Node.js"</em>).</li>
        <li><strong>Bullet points describing actions:</strong> Explain what you built, how you built it, and the results achieved. Use action verbs like <em>Developed</em>, <em>Implemented</em>, <em>Optimized</em>, or <em>Designed</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Quantify results:</strong> <em>"Developed a responsive front-end interface using React, reducing page load time by 20% through efficient asset lazy-loading."</em></li>
        <li><strong>Include a link:</strong> If the project is hosted on GitHub or a live URL, include the link next to the project title.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>How many projects to include?</strong> Aim for 2 to 4 projects. Each should have 3 to 4 bullet points. One well-documented project is always better than five vaguely described ones.</p>

      <h2>4. Categorize Your Skills Strategically</h2>
      <p>Recruiters look for specific technical keywords that match their job descriptions. To make your skills easy to scan and ATS-friendly, divide them into logical groups rather than listing 30 words in a single line:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Programming Languages:</strong> Java, Python, JavaScript, C++.</li>
        <li><strong>Libraries &amp; Frameworks:</strong> React.js, Node.js, Express, TailwindCSS.</li>
        <li><strong>Databases &amp; Tools:</strong> MySQL, MongoDB, Git, Postman, Docker.</li>
        <li><strong>Cloud &amp; DevOps:</strong> AWS (EC2, S3), Linux, CI/CD basics.</li>
        <li><strong>Soft Skills:</strong> Team collaboration, analytical thinking, verbal communication, agile methodology.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Only list skills you can genuinely discuss in an interview. If you list "Docker" but cannot explain what a container is, you will lose credibility instantly when questioned.</p>

      <h2>5. Proofread, Test, and Include Verified Links</h2>
      <p>Grammatical errors and spelling mistakes are the easiest way to get your resume rejected. They signal a lack of attention to detail — a fatal flaw for any technical role. Always proofread your resume multiple times using different methods:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Read it aloud to catch awkward phrasing and missing words.</li>
        <li>Use Grammarly or LanguageTool for automated grammar checks.</li>
        <li>Ask a peer, senior student, or mentor in your industry to review it.</li>
        <li>Print it out and proofread on paper — your brain reads differently in print.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Finally, ensure all links included — such as your LinkedIn profile, GitHub account, or personal portfolio — are active and accessible. Test each link from an incognito browser before submitting. A clickable GitHub link that showcases clean, documented code is worth more than a page of self-claimed achievements.</p>
      <p>Additionally, test how your resume looks when opened on different devices and PDF readers. What appears clean on your Mac may display differently on a recruiter's Windows PC.</p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <details>
        <summary><strong>Should freshers include a photo on their resume?</strong></summary>
        <p>In India, adding a professional passport-size photo is acceptable and common. However, in Western companies and MNCs operating globally, avoid photos to prevent unconscious bias. Check the company's norms and job location before adding your photo.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><strong>How far back should my education section go?</strong></summary>
        <p>List your graduation degree first (most recent), then your 12th standard board details, and then your 10th standard results. You do not need to go further back than 10th standard for a fresher resume.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><strong>Is it OK to apply without a cover letter?</strong></summary>
        <p>If a cover letter is listed as "optional," submitting one still gives you a significant edge. It takes 10 extra minutes and can make you stand out from 80% of applicants who skip it.</p>
      </details>

      <h2>Conclusion</h2>
      <p>A well-constructed fresher resume is the foundation of your entire job search. By following a clean layout, writing a targeted career objective, showcasing detailed project descriptions, categorizing your skills intelligently, and maintaining error-free content, you significantly improve your chances of landing interview calls. Remember: quality always beats quantity. One polished, tailored resume will outperform ten generic ones every single time.</p>
    `
